---
title: Nutrition algorithms
---

## nutrition-algorithm

Turns the user's goal plus their real intake and weight data into a daily
calorie target. Picked in **Nutrition → Goal → Algorithm**.

```ts
export const pluginBundle = {
  formatVersion: 1,
  pluginId: "com.you.pure-trend",
  family: "nutrition-algorithm",
  entryExport: "algorithm",
} as const;

export const algorithm = {
  id: "com.you.pure-trend",
  name: "Pure Trend",
  description: "Sets the target from intake vs. weight trend.",

  computeTargets(input) {
    // input: { goal, currentWeightKg, weightEntries, dailyIntakeKcal, userPreferences, now }
    const intake = input.dailyIntakeKcal;
    if (intake.length < 10) return { kcal: 0 };   // 0 = "not enough data"

    const avgIntake = intake.reduce((s, d) => s + d.kcal, 0) / intake.length;
    const rateKgPerWeek = input.goal.targetRateKgPerWeek ?? 0;
    const adjust = rateKgPerWeek * 1100; // ~7700 kcal/kg over a week

    return {
      kcal: Math.round(avgIntake + adjust),
      sourceLabel: "Trend",
      maintenanceKcal: Math.round(avgIntake),
    };
  },
};
```

### Output

```ts
type NutritionAlgorithmOutput = {
  kcal: number;                    // 0 signals "not enough data"
  macros?: { proteinG; carbsG; fatG };  // omit → app derives from the goal
  maintenanceKcal?: number;
  sourceLabel?: string;            // short badge, e.g. "Adaptive"
  notes?: string;
};
```

The app owns two things the algorithm doesn't: a manual calorie override on the
goal (always wins), and deriving macros from the goal's protein g/kg + fat % when
you return only `kcal`.

`preferencesSchema` / `defaultPreferences` work exactly as in
[progression](/docs/plugins/progression).

## nutrition-analytics

Same shape as [analytics](/docs/plugins/analytics) —
`compute(input) => { metrics, series, insights }` — but over a window of diary
days + weight entries:

```ts
compute(input) {
  // input: { days, weightEntries, goal, targets, range, now }
  const avgKcal =
    input.days.reduce((s, d) => s + (d.totals?.kcal ?? 0), 0) / (input.days.length || 1);
  return {
    metrics: [{ id: "avgKcal", value: Math.round(avgKcal) }],
    series: [],
    insights: [],
  };
}
```
